{"stories": [{"slug": "evidence", "content": "# Genealogical Analysis: R. P. Hamilton Obituary (May 9, 1912)\n\n## Reliability Assessment\n**Moderate-to-high.** Obituaries reflect informant-provided data, typically from family members who knew facts firsthand but may have omitted, misstated, or abbreviated details. The missing full name (\"R. P.\") is a significant limitation.\n\n## Direct Evidence\n- Death occurred Tuesday night, ~May 7, 1912, at home on North Broadway, Greensburg\n- Cause: stomach trouble\n- Survivors: wife (unnamed), daughter Miss Clara, sons Elmer (Greensburg) and Herschel (Oklahoma City)\n- Presbyterian church affiliation\n\n## Indirect Evidence\n- \"Miss Clara\" suggests she was unmarried in 1912\n- Herschel's Oklahoma City residence hints at westward family migration\n- \"Best known resident\" suggests long-term community presence — check city directories back to the 1880s–1890s\n\n## What It Proves vs. Suggests\n**Proves:** death date/location, family composition, religious affiliation. **Suggests:** approximate birth era (pre-1870s), possible Kansas location (Greensburg, KS fits Oklahoma City proximity).\n\n## Research Strategy\nResolve \"R. P.\" via death certificate, census records (1900, 1910), and Presbyterian church registers. Pursue Oklahoma probate records and Herschel's trail westward."}]}
